.he "mkfs (1)                     8/16/26                     mkfs (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
mkfs
- construct a file system
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
mkfs [ -f ] [ -i bootfile ] device [ size | -exclude ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Mkfs makes an empty file system on the block device.  The size, when
given, is the number of 512-byte blocks the file system is to hold; a
number written -exclude instead leaves that many blocks at the end of
the device, which is how swap space is kept out of the file system.
Given neither, the whole device is used.  The size of the device is
worked out from the drive, so it never has to be told.
.sp
Cylinder 0 is reserved for the boot and the disk label, whether or
not a boot is written.  The blocks are owned by a file, /boot/bootmw,
so the free list never hands them out.  The boot itself is read from
/bootmw.bin, or from bootfile with -i.
.sp
A disk that already holds a file system is left alone unless -f is
given.  The number of i-nodes is chosen from the size.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-f	Destroy a file system that is already there.

.ti -10
-i bootfile	Write bootfile into the boot area instead of /bootmw.bin.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
This is not the v7 mkfs: there is no prototype file, and it builds
only an empty file system.  The size is in blocks and may be given as
an exclusion, and the device size comes from the drive, not from the
command line.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
mknod (1), sync (1)
